From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 06:29:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Revert "arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Leave the PMC power
 state to the boot loader"

This reverts commit df99bbcc4c.

The J604 power-domain policy, t8132-j604-pmgr.dtsi, now keeps ps_pmc
unregistered together with every other domain decision, as
t8132-j713-pmgr.dtsi does for the J713. The single-domain override is
redundant. Leaving it in would also suggest that ps_pmc alone was
enough to hand the PMP over.

ps_pmc stays disabled in the built J604 device tree, so the PMP crash
that df99bbcc4c fixed (genpd gating the PMC before the PMP driver
loads) stays fixed at every point of this series.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts | 13 -------------
 1 file changed, 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
index e6a0e5451a..9708724549 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
@@ -703,19 +703,6 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
-/*
- * The PMC (the SoC's performance-state voter block at 0x382800000) is the
- * PMP firmware's: the PMP votes in it as voter agent 0 from its first
- * steps. The boot loader leaves it powered and Linux has no consumer for
- * it, so registering its power state would only let genpd gate it as unused
- * before the PMP driver loads, and the PMP firmware then crashes on its
- * first vote with an SError. Leave it as the boot loader left it, as the
- * J713 does.
- */
-&ps_pmc {
-	status = "disabled";
-};
-
 &aop {
 	status = "okay"; /* DTV-AUD-1, DTV-AUD-2, DTV-AUD-13 */
 };
